  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Buyer's Guide" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Casein Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Six micellar casein powders ranked on protein per scoop, independent testing and cost per 25 g, with an honest read of what the before-bed studies do and do not prove.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#what-is-casein">What Micellar Casein Is</a>
      <a href="#before-bed-evidence">The Before-Bed Evidence</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#how-we-ranked">How We Ranked</a>
      <a href="#side-by-side">Side by Side</a>
      <a href="#casein-vs-whey">Casein vs Whey</a>
      <a href="#dose-timing">How Much and When</a>
      <a href="#beyond-bedtime">Beyond Bedtime</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip Casein</a>
      <a href="#read-the-label">Reading a Casein Label</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Casein is the slow half of milk protein, and it has one genuinely useful trick: a shake before bed keeps amino acids trickling into your blood for hours while you sleep. The research says that helps mostly by adding protein to your day, not by unlocking extra gains on its own. So the best casein is simply the one that gives you the most clean, tested protein per dollar, in a texture you will actually drink at night.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-is-casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Casein Is, and Why Micellar Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cow's milk protein is roughly 80% casein and 20% whey. When milk is turned into cheese, the casein forms the curd and the whey drains off as liquid, which is why whey powder is cheap and plentiful while casein powders are a smaller category. Casein exists in milk as tiny clusters called micelles, bundles of protein held together with calcium and phosphate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Micellar casein is casein separated from milk while keeping those micelles largely intact, typically by filtering skim milk through membranes that hold back the large micelles and let lactose, minerals and whey proteins pass. That gentle process matters, because the intact micelle is what gives casein its slow-digesting behaviour. In the acidic environment of the stomach, micellar casein clots into a soft gel, which empties into the small intestine gradually rather than all at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other form you will see on labels is calcium caseinate or sodium caseinate. Caseinates are made by acidifying milk to precipitate casein and then neutralising it with an alkali, which breaks up the micelle structure. They mix more easily and are common in ready-to-drink shakes and blended powders, but they are generally considered less slow-digesting than micellar casein. If slow release is the reason you are buying casein, micellar casein should be the first ingredient on the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Micellar casein also carries a lot of calcium. NOW's unflavored micellar casein, for example, lists about 324 mg of calcium per 24 g scoop, roughly a quarter of the daily value. That is a small bonus rather than a reason to buy, but it is a real difference from whey isolate, which contains little calcium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture is the trade-off. Because micellar casein thickens as it hydrates, it mixes heavier than whey and can clump in a shaker bottle, which is one of the most common buyer complaints across every brand in this guide. Mixing with a blender or a little more liquid solves most of it, and some people deliberately use less liquid to make a spoonable protein pudding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-bed-evidence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What the Before-Bed Research Actually Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            The bedtime-casein idea comes mainly from Luc van Loon's group at Maastricht University. In a 2012 study, sixteen young men did an evening resistance-training session and then drank 40 g of casein about 30 minutes before sleep, or a placebo. The casein was digested and absorbed normally overnight, raised whole-body protein synthesis and improved overnight protein balance. Mixed muscle protein synthesis was about 22% higher with casein, but that result was only borderline statistically significant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Later work firmed up the dose question. A 2017 trial in 48 older men, average age around 72, compared 40 g of casein, 20 g of casein, 20 g of casein with added leucine, and a placebo before sleep. The authors concluded that 40 g increased overnight myofibrillar protein synthesis, and reviews by the same group have since suggested that around 40 g is needed for a robust overnight effect, with prior exercise helping more of that protein end up in muscle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best-known long-term trial, published by Snijders and colleagues in 2015, put 44 young men on a 12-week lifting programme and gave half of them a nightly drink with roughly 30 g of casein, while the other half got an energy-free placebo. The casein group gained more strength and more muscle size. The catch is that total daily protein was not matched: the casein group simply ate more protein and more calories, so the study cannot separate the bedtime timing from the extra protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            When researchers have matched total daily protein, the timing advantage largely disappears. Trials comparing casein taken before bed with the same casein taken earlier in the day, including an eight-week study in trained lifters and a ten-week study in recreational athletes, found no statistically significant difference in muscle or strength gains between the groups. A 2021 systematic review concluded that 20 to 40 g of casein before sleep improves the overnight protein synthetic response in healthy young men and may help muscle mass and strength over longer training blocks, while effects in older men were less clear.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST READ</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bedtime casein works mainly because it adds a protein meal.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a pre-sleep shake is the easiest way for you to reach your daily protein target, it is a good tool and casein is a sensible choice for it. If you already hit your target, moving protein to bedtime is unlikely to add much on its own.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            None of this makes casein special in a magic sense. It makes it practical. Many people find it hard to fit in one more protein feeding during the day, and the hours before bed are often free. A slow, thick, filling shake suits that slot, and the research says it does no harm to overnight protein balance and may help. That is the accurate case for buying casein, and it is a decent one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Casein Protein Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pick below is built on micellar casein, comes from a different brand and is sold through mainstream US retailers, with most available on Amazon. Nutrition figures are per the label or retailer listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the tub you buy. We have not tasted or lab-tested these ourselves; comments on taste and mixing are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Ranked These Casein Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with the casein powders that dominate US searches and Amazon listings, and kept only products whose main protein source is micellar casein. Blended powders that mix whey, caseinate and egg were excluded, because they do not offer the slow-digesting profile people buy casein for. We also required each product to be currently sold in the US with a published nutrition label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per serving and protein as a share of the scoop came first. A powder giving 24 to 26 g of protein from a 30 to 34 g scoop is roughly 70 to 85% protein by weight, which is what you should expect from a quality micellar casein. Lower ratios usually mean more flavoring, sweetener or filler. We also looked at calories, which should sit around 110 to 130 per serving for a flavored casein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Independent testing carried real weight. Informed Choice and NSF certifications screen for banned substances and, in NSF's case, verify that the label matches the contents. A brand saying its product is tested is less useful unless it names the programme or publishes results, so we report testing claims exactly as the brand or retailer states them and point you to the databases to verify your flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g of protein was the tie-breaker. We used typical online prices at the time of writing, rounded into ranges, because Amazon and retailer prices move week to week with Subscribe and Save offers and sales. Treat these figures as a comparison tool between products, not a price quote.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Casein Powders Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here are the label figures next to each other. Protein and calories are per serving, per the label or retailer listing at the time of writing, and vary by flavor. Cost per 25 g is an approximate range at typical online prices.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
