                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Choice (per brand)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.90-2.20</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Casein Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Third-party tested (per brand)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.85-1.10</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Sports Micellar Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand in-house lab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.00-1.45</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The spread in cost is wider than the spread in quality. Nutricost and NOW are the cheapest ways to buy micellar casein per gram, largely because the unflavored versions skip the flavoring and the marketing. Optimum Nutrition, Dymatize and Naked cluster in the middle, and Transparent Labs sits at the top, reflecting its grass-fed sourcing and certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein density is also tight across the field. Every product here delivers roughly 19 to 26 g of protein per serving, and the differences mostly reflect scoop size. NOW's smaller 24 g scoop gives 19 g, while Naked's 30 g double scoop gives 26 g. If you plan to follow the research dose of about 40 g before bed, any of these will need one and a half to two servings, which makes cost per gram more important than it first appears.
          </p>

          <p>
            Testing is where the products really differ. Dymatize and Transparent Labs carry Informed Choice claims, Naked cites NSF Contents Certified status for its unflavored powder, and Optimum Nutrition describes its casein as banned-substance tested. Nutricost and NOW rely on their own testing statements. If you are a drug-tested athlete, start with the certified options and confirm your flavor in the certifier's database.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="casein-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Casein vs Whey: Which Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The classic comparison is a 1997 study by Boirie and colleagues, which tracked amino acids in the blood after people drank whey or casein. Whey produced a fast, high spike that faded within about three to four hours. Casein produced a lower rise that lasted around seven hours. Whey stimulated protein synthesis more sharply, while casein did more to reduce protein breakdown over the longer window. That is the basis for calling whey fast and casein slow.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a shake right after training, whey is the better default. It is richer in leucine, at roughly 10 to 11% of the protein compared with about 8 to 9% for casein, and it delivers it quickly. It also mixes thinner and costs less per gram in most ranges. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> guide covers that category, and our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> comparison explains when the extra filtering is worth paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein earns its place when the gap before your next meal is long: overnight, before a long meeting, or when you want a shake that keeps you full. Its thickness and slower emptying from the stomach make it noticeably more filling for many people, which is one reason it appears in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">best protein powder for weight loss</a> picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Across a whole day the difference shrinks. When total daily protein is matched, studies generally find similar muscle gains whether people use whey, casein or a mix. If you can only buy one tub, whey is the more versatile choice. If you already own whey and want to add a bedtime or between-meals shake, casein is the logical second tub. For the broader picture of building muscle with supplements, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">best protein powder for muscle gain</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milk itself is a blend of both, which is why a glass of milk or a bowl of Greek yogurt or cottage cheese, both casein-heavy foods, can do the same bedtime job as a shake. Cottage cheese in particular is mostly casein. A powder is simply more convenient and lower in fat and lactose than the whole-food route.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dose-timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Casein to Take, and When
          </h2>

          <p>
            The research dose for a measurable overnight effect is about 40 g of casein, taken roughly 30 minutes before sleep. For most products in this guide that is one and a half to two servings. Some studies in younger men also found benefits with around 30 g, so if 40 g feels like too much at night, 30 g is a reasonable middle ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            Daily total matters more than the bedtime dose. For people who lift, a daily intake of around 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight is a widely cited target, and a casein shake is just one way to reach it. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">daily protein guide</a> and <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will give you a personal number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing during the day is flexible. Casein works between meals, as a mid-afternoon snack, or with breakfast if you know you will not eat again for five or six hours. Our <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">guide to protein timing</a> explains when timing matters and when it does not. The one situation where whey is clearly better is immediately after training, when a faster rise in amino acids is the goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Older adults may have the strongest case for bedtime casein. Ageing muscle responds less to small protein doses, and the 2017 trial in older men found that 40 g, rather than 20 g, was the dose that clearly raised overnight muscle protein synthesis. Anyone with kidney disease, or anyone taking medication or managing a medical condition, should discuss protein targets with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding supplements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="beyond-bedtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Beyond Bedtime: Puddings, Baking and Staying Full
          </h2>

          <p>
            Casein's thickness, a nuisance in a shaker bottle, is an asset in the kitchen. Mixed with a small amount of milk or water and stirred, a scoop of micellar casein turns into a thick pudding or mousse that many people eat with a spoon. Chill it for half an hour and the texture firms further. This is the single most common way buyers say they make casein enjoyable at night.
          </p>

          <p>
            In baking, casein holds moisture better than whey, which tends to dry out and toughen muffins and pancakes. Unflavored casein, such as Naked, Nutricost or NOW, can replace part of the flour in many recipes. Because casein absorbs a lot of liquid, you usually need a little extra milk, yogurt or egg to keep the batter workable.
          </p>

          <p>
            For appetite control, casein's slower digestion is a practical advantage. A thick casein shake or pudding between meals or after dinner can curb grazing for many people, and pairing it with fruit or a little nut butter makes it a more complete snack. It will not cause fat loss on its own, but it can make a calorie deficit easier to live with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein also suits people who want to cut down on liquid before bed. Because a pudding needs only a few ounces of liquid, it is gentler on anyone who wakes to use the bathroom at night. If a full shake before bed disrupts your sleep, try the pudding method or take your casein an hour or two earlier in the evening.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip Casein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid casein entirely. Casein is one of the main allergenic proteins in milk, and micellar casein is concentrated milk protein, so it is not suitable for people with a diagnosed cow's milk allergy. Vegans and anyone avoiding dairy for other reasons should look at plant proteins instead; our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide includes several organic pea and hemp options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose intolerance is a different issue. Micellar casein is filtered to remove most lactose, and products like Dymatize and ON list only a few grams of carbohydrate per serving, so many lactose-intolerant people tolerate it in normal amounts. If you are very sensitive, start with a half serving, or take a lactase supplement with it, and see how you feel.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also skip casein if you already reach your daily protein target from food. The research does not support casein as a must-have; it supports it as a convenient way to add protein. If your diet already covers your needs, an extra tub is optional, and the money may be better spent on food quality or a good whey for after training.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be wary of casein if you suffer from heartburn or reflux at night. A large, thick shake right before lying down does not suit everyone. Taking it an hour or more before bed, or using a smaller dose, may help. If you have ongoing digestive symptoms, see a doctor rather than self-treating with supplements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="read-the-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Read a Casein Label Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the first ingredient. It should say micellar casein. If it says protein blend, look at the breakdown: blends that list whey concentrate, calcium caseinate and milk protein ahead of micellar casein are not true slow-release casein powders, even if casein is in the product name. Blends are fine as general protein, just not what this guide is about.
          </p>

          <p>
            Divide protein by serving weight. A quality micellar casein should be about 70 to 85% protein by weight; ON lists 24 g in about 34 g, Naked 26 g in about 30 g. A lower ratio means more of the scoop is flavoring, sweetener or other ingredients. It is a quick way to judge value between flavors, too, because chocolate and cookie flavors often have slightly lower protein per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for a named certification rather than a vague claim. Informed Choice, Informed Sport and NSF Certified for Sport all have public databases searchable by product and flavor. A Certificate of Analysis from an accredited lab is useful, but only if it is recent and for the product you are buying. Our brand reviews of <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition</a>, <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize</a>, <a href="/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Naked Nutrition</a> and <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs</a> go deeper on each brand's range and testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, on Amazon, buy from the brand's own storefront or from listings shipped and sold by Amazon where you can. Supplement counterfeits and expired stock do turn up from third-party sellers. Check the seller name, compare the label photo to the brand's site and avoid listings priced far below every other retailer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a casein outside this list, Legion's Casein+ is another micellar option sold mainly on the brand's own site. Legion describes it as Truly Grass Fed certified micellar casein from Ireland and says it is lab-tested with a published certificate of analysis. We left it out of the main picks because its listed protein per serving varied between sources at the time of writing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying casein to replace your post-workout whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Casein digests slowly and has a little less leucine than whey, so it is the weaker choice right after training. Use whey after workouts and casein for long gaps such as overnight. If you can only afford one tub, buy whey and use milk, yogurt or cottage cheese for bedtime protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking a 20 g bedtime dose and expecting the research result</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The overnight muscle studies mostly used around 40 g of casein, and in older men 20 g did not produce the same clear effect. One scoop of most powders gives 19 to 26 g. If bedtime protein is your plan, take one and a half to two servings, or pair a scoop with Greek yogurt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a blend labeled casein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some powders put casein on the front but list whey concentrate, calcium caseinate or milk protein first in the ingredients. Those blends digest faster and are not true slow-release products. If slow release is why you are buying, make sure micellar casein is the first ingredient, not a minor part of a blend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing casein like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Micellar casein thickens as it hydrates and clumps in a shaker with too little liquid. Add powder to liquid rather than the other way round, use at least 10 to 12 oz for a shake, or use a blender. Better still, use less liquid on purpose and eat it as a pudding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting bedtime casein to beat a good daily total</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When total daily protein is matched, studies find little extra benefit from moving casein to bedtime. A pre-sleep shake helps mainly by adding protein. If you are already eating enough, the shake is optional, and your money may do more for you as better food or a whey for training days.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best casein protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein is the best all-round pick: 24 g of micellar casein per scoop at about 120 calories, banned-substance tested per the brand, and easy to find. Dymatize Elite Casein suits tested athletes, Naked Casein is the best unflavored option, and Nutricost is the budget choice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does taking casein before bed build more muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help, mainly by adding protein to your day. Studies show about 40 g of casein before sleep is digested overnight and can raise overnight muscle protein synthesis. But when total daily protein is matched, trials comparing bedtime and daytime casein have found little difference in muscle gains. Hitting your daily target matters most.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much casein should I take before bed?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The research dose is about 40 g, taken roughly 30 minutes before sleep, which is one and a half to two servings of most powders. Around 30 g has also shown benefits in younger adults. If you have kidney disease or a medical condition, check protein targets with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a nightly shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the difference between micellar casein and calcium caseinate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Micellar casein is filtered from milk with its natural micelle structure intact, which is why it clots in the stomach and digests slowly. Calcium caseinate is made by acid-precipitating casein and neutralising it, which breaks up the micelles. Caseinate mixes more easily but is generally considered less slow-digesting, so choose micellar casein for bedtime use.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein better than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither is better overall; they suit different times. Whey digests fast and is richer in leucine, so it is the better post-workout choice and usually cheaper per gram. Casein digests slowly and is more filling, so it suits bedtime and long gaps between meals. Across a full day with matched protein, muscle gains tend to be similar.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I take casein if I am lactose intolerant?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Often, yes. Micellar casein is filtered to remove most lactose, and most products list only a few grams of carbohydrate per serving. Start with half a serving to test your tolerance. Casein is not suitable for anyone with a cow's milk allergy, though, because casein is one of the main milk allergens, so check with a doctor if unsure.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can casein help with weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Indirectly. Casein's thick texture and slow digestion make it more filling than many protein shakes, which can make it easier to avoid evening snacking while eating in a calorie deficit. It does not burn fat on its own. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">weight-loss protein guide</a> covers how to fit it into a lower-calorie diet.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

